# Selecto Window Functions Workbook

## Setup 1) Install Dependencies

Run this cell first. It installs the local `selecto_livebooks` project and its
current Selecto dependencies for this Livebook session.

```elixir
Code.require_file(Path.expand("support/bootstrap.exs", __DIR__))
SelectoLivebooksNotebookBootstrap.install!()
```

## Overview

This workbook focuses on analytical window functions that keep row-level detail while adding per-partition metrics.

1. Department ranking with `row_number`
2. Department average with `avg(...) over (...)`
3. Running totals with ordered windows
4. Previous/next comparisons with `lag` and `lead`
5. Salary banding with `ntile`

```mermaid
flowchart LR
  base[Base row selection]
  partition[Define partition]
  order[Define order within partition]
  win[Apply window function]
  result[Row + analytic columns]

  base --> partition --> order --> win --> result
```

```mermaid
graph TD
  dept[department partition]
  emp1[employee row]
  emp2[employee row]
  emp3[employee row]
  metric[window metric per row]

  dept --> emp1 --> metric
  dept --> emp2 --> metric
  dept --> emp3 --> metric
```

Before running, initialize the sample database:

```bash
mix setup
```

## Setup 2) Connect to Repo and Build Employee Domain

This cell defines a local Repo and an employee-focused domain for window function demos.

```elixir
defmodule WindowLab.Repo do
  use Ecto.Repo,
    otp_app: :window_lab_livebook,
    adapter: Ecto.Adapters.Postgres
end

defmodule WindowLab.EmployeeDomain do
  @moduledoc false

  def domain do
    %{
      name: "Employees",
      source: %{
        source_table: "employees",
        primary_key: :id,
        fields: [
          :id,
          :first_name,
          :last_name,
          :email,
          :title,
          :department,
          :salary,
          :hire_date,
          :active,
          :manager_id,
          :inserted_at
        ],
        columns: %{
          id: %{type: :integer},
          first_name: %{type: :string},
          last_name: %{type: :string},
          email: %{type: :string},
          title: %{type: :string},
          department: %{type: :string},
          salary: %{type: :decimal},
          hire_date: %{type: :date},
          active: %{type: :boolean},
          manager_id: %{type: :integer},
          inserted_at: %{type: :utc_datetime}
        },
        associations: %{}
      },
      schemas: %{},
      joins: %{},
      default_selected: ["first_name", "last_name", "department", "salary"],
      default_order_by: [{"department", :asc}, {"salary", :desc}]
    }
  end
end

repo_config = [
  database: System.get_env("SELECTO_LIVEBOOKS_DB", "selecto_livebooks_dev"),
  username: System.get_env("SELECTO_LIVEBOOKS_DB_USER", "postgres"),
  password: System.get_env("SELECTO_LIVEBOOKS_DB_PASS", "postgres"),
  hostname: System.get_env("SELECTO_LIVEBOOKS_DB_HOST", "localhost"),
  port: String.to_integer(System.get_env("SELECTO_LIVEBOOKS_DB_PORT", "5432")),
  pool_size: 5
]

case Process.whereis(WindowLab.Repo) do
  nil -> {:ok, _pid} = WindowLab.Repo.start_link(repo_config)
  _pid -> :ok
end

config = %{
  repo: WindowLab.Repo,
  employee_domain: WindowLab.EmployeeDomain.domain()
}

{:ok, result} = Ecto.Adapters.SQL.query(config.repo, "select count(*) from employees", [])
[[employee_count]] = result.rows
IO.puts("Connected. Employees in dataset: #{employee_count}")

config
```

## Setup 3) Define Window Helpers

This helper cell prints SQL/params and executes a query with a compact preview.

```elixir
defmodule WindowLab.Helpers do
  @moduledoc false

  def explain(label, query) do
    Selecto.Livebook.explain(label, query)
  end

  def run(label, query, preview_count \\ 10) do
    Selecto.Livebook.run(label, query, preview_count: preview_count)
  end

  def run_shape(label, query, preview_count \\ 10) do
    Selecto.Livebook.run_shape(label, query, preview_count: preview_count)
  end
end
```

## 1) Department Salary Rank + Department Average

This query shows each active employee row and adds two analytics:

1. `department_salary_rank` from highest salary to lowest within each department
2. `department_avg_salary` repeated on each row for quick comparison

```elixir
department_rank_query =
  Selecto.configure(config.employee_domain, config.repo)
  |> Selecto.filter({"active", true})
  |> Selecto.select([
    "first_name",
    "last_name",
    "department",
    "salary"
  ])
  |> Selecto.window_function(:row_number, [],
    over: [partition_by: ["department"], order_by: [{"salary", :desc}]],
    as: "department_salary_rank"
  )
  |> Selecto.window_function(:avg, ["salary"],
    over: [partition_by: ["department"]],
    as: "department_avg_salary"
  )
  |> Selecto.order_by({"department", :asc})
  |> Selecto.order_by({"salary", :desc})
  |> Selecto.limit(20)

WindowLab.Helpers.run("Department rank + average", department_rank_query)
```

## 2) Running Department Payroll by Salary Order

This query adds cumulative payroll at each row inside a department, ordered by salary descending.

```elixir
running_total_query =
  Selecto.configure(config.employee_domain, config.repo)
  |> Selecto.filter({"active", true})
  |> Selecto.select([
    "first_name",
    "last_name",
    "department",
    "salary"
  ])
  |> Selecto.window_function(:sum, ["salary"],
    over: [partition_by: ["department"], order_by: [{"salary", :desc}]],
    as: "running_department_payroll"
  )
  |> Selecto.order_by({"department", :asc})
  |> Selecto.order_by({"salary", :desc})
  |> Selecto.limit(20)

WindowLab.Helpers.run("Running department payroll", running_total_query)
```

## 3) Previous and Next Salary in the Same Department

`lag` and `lead` let you compare each salary row with nearby rows in the same ordered partition.

```elixir
neighbor_compare_query =
  Selecto.configure(config.employee_domain, config.repo)
  |> Selecto.filter({"active", true})
  |> Selecto.select([
    "first_name",
    "last_name",
    "department",
    "salary"
  ])
  |> Selecto.window_function(:lag, ["salary", 1],
    over: [partition_by: ["department"], order_by: [{"salary", :desc}]],
    as: "prev_salary"
  )
  |> Selecto.window_function(:lead, ["salary", 1],
    over: [partition_by: ["department"], order_by: [{"salary", :desc}]],
    as: "next_salary"
  )
  |> Selecto.order_by({"department", :asc})
  |> Selecto.order_by({"salary", :desc})
  |> Selecto.limit(20)

WindowLab.Helpers.run("Lag/lead salary neighbors", neighbor_compare_query)
```

## 4) Salary Quartiles with `ntile`

`ntile(4)` assigns each row into a quartile bucket based on global salary ordering.

```elixir
quartile_query =
  Selecto.configure(config.employee_domain, config.repo)
  |> Selecto.filter({"active", true})
  |> Selecto.select([
    "first_name",
    "last_name",
    "department",
    "salary"
  ])
  |> Selecto.window_function(:ntile, [4],
    over: [order_by: [{"salary", :desc}]],
    as: "salary_quartile"
  )
  |> Selecto.order_by({"salary", :desc})
  |> Selecto.limit(30)

WindowLab.Helpers.run("Global salary quartiles", quartile_query)
```

## 6) Percentiles and Moving Averages

Window functions can express percentile-style buckets and moving averages while
preserving employee rows.

```elixir
percentile_moving_average_query =
  Selecto.configure(config.employee_domain, config.repo)
  |> Selecto.filter({"active", true})
  |> Selecto.select(["first_name", "last_name", "department", "salary", "hire_date"])
  |> Selecto.window_function(:percent_rank, [],
    over: [partition_by: ["department"], order_by: [{"salary", :asc}]],
    as: "department_salary_percent_rank"
  )
  |> Selecto.window_function(:avg, ["salary"],
    over: [
      partition_by: ["department"],
      order_by: [{"hire_date", :asc}],
      frame: {:rows, {:preceding, 2}, :current_row}
    ],
    as: "three_hire_moving_avg_salary"
  )
  |> Selecto.order_by({"department", :asc})
  |> Selecto.order_by({"hire_date", :asc})
  |> Selecto.limit(30)

WindowLab.Helpers.run("Percent rank and moving average", percentile_moving_average_query)
```

## 7) Structured Output with Window Columns

Package row fields and analytic fields into a nested shape for dashboards or API
payloads.

```elixir
window_shape_query =
  Selecto.configure(config.employee_domain, config.repo)
  |> Selecto.filter({"active", true})
  |> Selecto.select_shape([
    {"first_name", "last_name"},
    "department",
    {
      "salary",
      {:field,
       {:raw_sql, "ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY selecto_root.department ORDER BY selecto_root.salary DESC)"},
       "department_rank"}
    }
  ])
  |> Selecto.order_by({"department", :asc})
  |> Selecto.order_by({"salary", :desc})
  |> Selecto.limit(20)

WindowLab.Helpers.run_shape("Window output shape", window_shape_query)
```

## 8) Department and Tenure Dashboard Slice

Compare top earners by department and a derived tenure bucket.

```elixir
tenure_dashboard_query =
  Selecto.configure(config.employee_domain, config.repo)
  |> Selecto.filter({"active", true})
  |> Selecto.select(["first_name", "last_name", "department", "salary", "hire_date"])
  |> Selecto.case_when_select([
    {[{"hire_date", {:<, ~D[2019-01-01]}}], "senior tenure"},
    {[{"hire_date", {:<, ~D[2023-01-01]}}], "mid tenure"}
  ], else: "newer tenure", as: "tenure_bucket")
  |> Selecto.window_function(:row_number, [],
    over: [partition_by: ["department"], order_by: [{"salary", :desc}]],
    as: "department_top_earner_rank"
  )
  |> Selecto.order_by({"department", :asc})
  |> Selecto.order_by({"salary", :desc})
  |> Selecto.limit(50)

WindowLab.Helpers.run("Top earners by department and tenure", tenure_dashboard_query)
```

## 9) Row vs Range Frame Comparison

Compare row-count frames with value-range frames for moving salary totals.

```elixir
{:ok, frame_comparison} =
  Ecto.Adapters.SQL.query(config.repo, """
  SELECT
    department,
    first_name,
    last_name,
    salary,
    sum(salary) OVER (
      PARTITION BY department
      ORDER BY salary
      ROWS BETWEEN 2 PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW
    ) AS rows_frame_total,
    sum(salary) OVER (
      PARTITION BY department
      ORDER BY salary
      RANGE BETWEEN 10000 PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW
    ) AS range_frame_total
  FROM employees
  ORDER BY department, salary
  LIMIT 25
  """, [])

frame_comparison.rows
```

## 10) CTE Pre-Aggregation with Window Functions

Pre-aggregate each department, then apply a window rank to the summarized rows.

```elixir
{:ok, department_salary_rank} =
  Ecto.Adapters.SQL.query(config.repo, """
  WITH department_summary AS (
    SELECT department, count(*) AS employee_count, avg(salary) AS average_salary, sum(salary) AS total_salary
    FROM employees
    GROUP BY department
  )
  SELECT
    department,
    employee_count,
    average_salary,
    total_salary,
    dense_rank() OVER (ORDER BY average_salary DESC) AS average_salary_rank
  FROM department_summary
  ORDER BY average_salary_rank, department
  """, [])

department_salary_rank.rows
```

## 11) Salary Distribution Chart Data by Department Rank

Build a chart-ready dataset from department salary ranks. The cell returns a
VegaLite-compatible spec and falls back to a table when VegaLite is unavailable.

```elixir
chart_values =
  Enum.map(department_salary_rank.rows, fn [department, employee_count, average_salary, total_salary, rank] ->
    %{
      department: department,
      employee_count: employee_count,
      average_salary: Decimal.to_float(average_salary),
      total_salary: Decimal.to_float(total_salary),
      rank: rank
    }
  end)

salary_rank_spec = %{
  "$schema" => "https://vega.github.io/schema/vega-lite/v5.json",
  "data" => %{"values" => chart_values},
  "mark" => "bar",
  "encoding" => %{
    "x" => %{"field" => "department", "type" => "nominal", "sort" => "-y"},
    "y" => %{"field" => "average_salary", "type" => "quantitative"},
    "color" => %{"field" => "rank", "type" => "ordinal"}
  }
}

if Code.ensure_loaded?(VegaLite) do
  VegaLite.from_json(Jason.encode!(salary_rank_spec))
else
  Kino.DataTable.new(chart_values)
end
```

## 12) Interactive Frame and Department Controls

Use input-style values to change the moving frame size and department filter.

```elixir
frame_size_input = Kino.Input.number("Rows preceding", default: 2)
department_input = Kino.Input.text("Department", default: "Engineering")

read_or = fn input, default ->
  try do
    Kino.Input.read(input) || default
  rescue
    _ -> default
  end
end

frame_size = read_or.(frame_size_input, 2)
department_filter = read_or.(department_input, "Engineering")

{:ok, controlled_frame_rows} =
  Ecto.Adapters.SQL.query(config.repo, """
  SELECT
    department,
    first_name,
    last_name,
    salary,
    avg(salary) OVER (
      PARTITION BY department
      ORDER BY salary
      ROWS BETWEEN #{frame_size} PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW
    ) AS moving_average
  FROM employees
  WHERE department = $1
  ORDER BY salary
  LIMIT 20
  """, [department_filter])

controlled_frame_rows.rows
```

## 13) Percentile and Distribution Functions

Add distribution windows alongside rank functions.

```elixir
{:ok, distribution_rows} =
  Ecto.Adapters.SQL.query(config.repo, """
  SELECT
    department,
    first_name,
    last_name,
    salary,
    percent_rank() OVER (PARTITION BY department ORDER BY salary) AS percent_rank,
    cume_dist() OVER (PARTITION BY department ORDER BY salary) AS cumulative_distribution,
    ntile(4) OVER (PARTITION BY department ORDER BY salary) AS salary_quartile
  FROM employees
  ORDER BY department, salary
  LIMIT 30
  """, [])

distribution_rows.rows
```

## 14) Cumulative Salary Curve Spec

Build chart-ready values for cumulative salary curves by department.

```elixir
{:ok, cumulative_salary_rows} =
  Ecto.Adapters.SQL.query(config.repo, """
  SELECT
    department,
    salary,
    sum(salary) OVER (PARTITION BY department ORDER BY salary) AS cumulative_salary
  FROM employees
  ORDER BY department, salary
  """, [])

cumulative_values =
  Enum.map(cumulative_salary_rows.rows, fn [department, salary, cumulative_salary] ->
    %{department: department, salary: Decimal.to_float(salary), cumulative_salary: Decimal.to_float(cumulative_salary)}
  end)

%{
  mark: "line",
  data: %{values: cumulative_values},
  encoding: %{
    x: %{field: "salary", type: "quantitative"},
    y: %{field: "cumulative_salary", type: "quantitative"},
    color: %{field: "department", type: "nominal"}
  }
}
```

